      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Place the chicken thighs in a large saucepan with 1.5 litres of cold water. Bring to a boil. Skim off the foam. Reduce to a simmer and cook for 25 minutes. Remove the chicken and strain the broth. Return the broth to the pan with the dashi stock. Keep warm over a low heat."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "While the broth simmers, cook the minced pork. Heat the vegetable oil in a frying pan over a medium-high heat. Add the garlic and ginger. Cook for 30 seconds. Add the pork mince and break it up. Cook for 5 to 6 minutes until browned and cooked through. Add a splash of soy sauce and the sake. Stir and set aside."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Make the miso tare. In a small bowl, whisk together the white miso, soy sauce and mirin until smooth. It should be the consistency of thick cream."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Cook the noodles in a separate pan of boiling water following the packet instructions. Drain well."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add about 2 tablespoons of the miso tare to each warmed bowl. Add a few drops of sesame oil. Ladle the hot broth over. Stir to combine."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add a portion of noodles to each bowl. Top with the pork mince, sweetcorn, spring onions and sesame seeds. Serve immediately."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "attrs": {
          "items": [
            {
              "symptom": "The broth tastes thin despite using plenty of miso",
              "cause": "The tare was not stirred in well before adding the broth",
              "fix": "Whisk the tare thoroughly in the bowl first, then add a small amount of hot broth and whisk to loosen it before adding the rest."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "The miso flavour is bitter",
              "cause": "The miso was boiled with the broth",
              "fix": "Remove the pan from the heat before adding any miso. Miso must never come to a boil. Always add tare to the bowl and pour broth on top."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "The noodles have gone soft and starchy in the bowl",
              "cause": "Noodles were cooked too far in advance",
              "fix": "Cook the noodles last, just before serving. Rinse them briefly under hot water after draining to remove excess starch, then add immediately to the waiting broth."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Miso ramen was born in Sapporo, Hokkaido in the 1950s. The cold winters of the north called for a broth with more body and heat than the southern shio and shoyu styles could provide. Hokkaido miso, made from locally grown soybeans and corn, became the defining topping combination. Today it is one of the most popular ramen styles in Japan and the one most home cooks in the UK can replicate with widely available ingredients."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
